<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arnulf of Carinthia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 850–899</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-dynasty">Carolingian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arnulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a> was an illegitimate Carolingian who deposed his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-fat">Charles the Fat</a> in 887 and became king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a>. He defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dyle">Dyle</a> in 891, fought Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> and was crowned emperor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 896. He made his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zwentibold">Zwentibold</a> king of Lotharingia in 895. After his death <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyar</a> raids devastated the kingdom.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arnulf-of-carinthia.jpg" alt="Arnulf of Carinthia" width="236" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_of_Arnulph_of_Carinthia_(896).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Arnulf was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carloman-of-bavaria">Carloman of Bavaria</a> and Liutswind. West Frankish writers call him illegitimate, but it is possible that Carloman later married Arnulf's mother, which would have legitimised him. His father gave him the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a>, and he spent his childhood at Moosburg in Carinthia, which was close to the Carolingian palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnburg">Karnburg</a>. After a stroke incapacitated Carloman in 879, Arnulf was confirmed in Carinthia. In 882 he supported Engelschalk II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> in the Wilhelminer War, which ruined his relationship with his uncle, Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-fat">Charles the Fat</a>, and set him at war with Svatopluk of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In November 887 a diet at Tribur met, and Arnulf, supported by nobles from the eastern parts of the empire, deposed the ailing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-fat">Charles the Fat</a> and was proclaimed king. Charles died at the start of 888. Regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-francia">West Francia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> elected their own rulers, though Odo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> formally accepted Arnulf's suzerainty. In 891 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> crushed an East Frankish army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maastricht">Maastricht</a>, but in September Arnulf repelled them, and the Annales Fuldenses report that the dead Northmen blocked the river. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diet-of-worms">Diet of Worms</a> in May 895 he crowned his illegitimate son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zwentibold">Zwentibold</a> king of Lotharingia.</p><p>Arnulf failed to conquer Great Moravia in 892, 893 and 899, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> broke away from Moravia in 895 and became his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>. In 894, at the request of Pope Formosus, he invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and took Pavia. He returned in 895 and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> by force on 21 February 896, and Formosus crowned him emperor on 22 February. A stroke then forced him to return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, and he kept power in Italy only while he was there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the stroke Arnulf also suffered from an infestation of lice on his eyelid, which prevented him from dealing with his problems. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> was lost, raiders from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> harassed his lands, and Lotharingia revolted against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zwentibold">Zwentibold</a>. He died at Regensburg on 8 December 899 and is entombed in St. Emmeram's Basilica. His only legitimate son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-child">Louis the Child</a>, succeeded him, and when Louis died in 911 the East Frankish branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-dynasty">Carolingian dynasty</a> ceased to exist. In his last year Arnulf had sought help from the Magyars against Moravia, and they were then settled in the Carpathian Basin. Zwentibold ruled Lotharingia until his murder in 900.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Arnulf of Carinthia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Arnulf of Carinthia</b>, king of East Francia, invaded Italy again and was crowned emperor in Rome by Pope Formosus, but soon went back to East Francia.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-22">22 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>His nephew <b>Arnulf of Carinthia</b> became king of East Francia, and the empire fragmented permanently.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-17">17 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Formosus's reign was troubled by power struggles over Constantinople, West Francia and the Holy Roman Empire, in which he sided with <b>Arnulf of Carinthia</b> against Lambert of Spoleto.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-6">6 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Later, Duke Braslav held lands between the Drava and Sava and aided the East Frankish king <b>Arnulf</b> against Great Moravia in the 880s and 890s.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-pannonian-croatia">Duchy of Pannonian Croatia</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Arnulf of Carinthia</b> defeated the Vikings on the Dyle in 891 and was crowned emperor in 896, but Magyar raids devastated the kingdom after 900.</blockquote><a href="/state/east-francia">East Francia</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Arnulf of Carinthia</b> made his son Zwentibold king in 895, but he was killed in 900, and the land became the Duchy of Lotharingia.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-lotharingia">Kingdom of Lotharingia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Regensburg, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.02,12.08&amp;year=881" aria-label="Regensburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Arnulf of Carinthia"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5673 5872h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5673 5872h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=49.02,12.08&amp;year=881">What was here in 881 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
